<interrogationes unitas="tls">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I open an HTTPS-style connection, send a request and read the reply?
    <responsum symbolum="tls_connectere tls_mittere_omnia tls_recipere tls_claudere">Call
      tls_connectere(hospes, portus, piscina) with a DNS name (it is
      sent as SNI and checked against the certificate) and test
      .successus; on failure .error and .error_descriptio say why. Send
      with tls_mittere_omnia, then loop tls_recipere until it returns
      0 (clean EOF) or a negative value, and always finish with
      tls_claudere, which frees the context and closes the TCP socket
      and may be called twice. Most callers want http.h instead.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I set a timeout, trust a private CA, or turn off certificate checks?
    <responsum symbolum="tls_optiones_default tls_connectere_cum_optionibus TlsOptiones">Start
      from tls_optiones_default() (timeout_ms XXX * M, verification on,
      system trust store) and pass it to tls_connectere_cum_optionibus.
      ancora_via names a DER CA certificate that becomes the ONLY trust
      anchor (chain, dates and host name are still checked).
      verificare_certificatum FALSUM skips the trust decision. A
      zero-filled TlsOptiones selects the default transport but also
      means no verification.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I get a connection with no timeout by setting timeout_ms to 0?
    <responsum symbolum="TlsOptiones tcp_optiones_default">No,
      despite the header comment. Every transport copies timeout_ms
      into the TCP options only when it is greater than 0, so 0 leaves
      the TCP default from tcp_optiones_default, which is XXX * M ms
      (30 seconds). Pass a large value if you need a long wait.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is a TLS vectura and which one do I get?
    <responsum symbolum="tls_vecturam_eligere tls_vectura tls_versio TlsVectura">The
      backend that carries the connection. TlsOptiones.vectura wins
      when not ORDINARIA; otherwise the RHUBARB_TLS environment
      variable (bearssl, securetransport or tls13), read on every
      connection; otherwise the house default, the in-house TLS 1.3
      client. tls_vecturam_eligere tells you the choice in advance,
      tls_vectura the backend actually in use, tls_versio the
      negotiated version (0x0304 or 0x0303, 0 before the
      handshake).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does tls_vectura say BEARSSL when I asked for the TLS 1.3 client?
    <responsum symbolum="tls_vectura tls_versio tls_tls13_tcp_relinquere" via="lib/tls_tls13.worklog.md project-specs/tls13-spec.md">The
      server answered as TLS 1.2 only. The 1.3 client then hands the
      socket back, the dispatcher reconnects on the same fd number and
      finishes with BearSSL TLS 1.2, checking the DOWNGRD sentinel in
      the server random so a downgrade attack is refused. Set
      sine_descensu VERUM to get TLS_ERROR_HANDSHAKE instead of the
      fallback.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I do a non-blocking TLS handshake inside an event loop?
    <responsum symbolum="tls_incipere tls_gradus tls_gradus_error tcp_incipere tcp_incipere_perficere" via="lib/http_vocatio.c">Connect
      TCP yourself (tcp_incipere, then tcp_incipere_perficere), then
      call tls_incipere; nothing is sent yet. Call tls_gradus whenever
      the socket is ready: LEGERE or SCRIBERE says what to wait for
      next, PERFECTUS means done, ERROR means read tls_gradus_error.
      The TLS connection owns the TcpConnexio from then on, and on
      failure tls_incipere has already closed it, so do not close it
      again (http_vocatio.c is the real example).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I tell a receive timeout apart from would-block when tls_recipere returns -2?
    <responsum symbolum="tls_recipere tls_mittere">Not
      by the value: TLS_ITERUM and TCP_ITERUM are both -2. On a
      blocking connection (tls_connectere) it means the receive
      timeout expired; on a stepped one (tls_incipere) it means nothing
      is ready now. A stepped caller must keep reading until
      TLS_ITERUM on each wakeup, because decrypted bytes can be held
      inside the backend where the socket no longer signals them.
      Only 0 means the peer closed; -1 is an error.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I send a partial buffer with tls_mittere and continue later?
    <responsum symbolum="tls_mittere tls_mittere_omnia">Yes:
      tls_mittere returns how many bytes it took (or -1); on TLS_ITERUM
      call it again with the SAME remaining bytes once the socket is
      writable. tls_mittere_omnia loops for you, sleeping 1 ms on 0 or
      TLS_ITERUM, with no overall deadline: on a blocking connection a
      send that times out also comes back as TLS_ITERUM, so a peer that
      stops reading keeps it looping. It returns FALSUM for an empty
      buffer (mensura 0), the same answer as an error.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I read the server certificate's expiry date?
    <responsum symbolum="tls_certificatum_obtinere TlsCertificatum">Call
      tls_certificatum_obtinere while the connection is still open
      (after tls_claudere nothing is left). It fills a TlsCertificatum
      with the leaf's notBefore and notAfter as Unix epoch seconds and
      the subject CN as a chorda allocated in the connection's piscina;
      days left = (non_post - now) / 86400. FALSUM, with valida FALSUM,
      when the certificate could not be read.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
